On Wed, 06 Dec 2006 00:07:33 +0100, Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com>
wrote:

> I don't see a lot of duplicated effort in the development of Nethack
> patches. Where do you see this effort?


Look at some of the patch collection sites and note the overlap of
work and concepts.  If you read enough of r.g.r.n, you'll fairly often
see people announcing that they've made some enhancement, and someone
else will say they already did a similar thing X years ago.

The lack of central coordination and a central repository results in
many coding conflicts and abandoned projects.  This is magnified at
the time of each official nethack release, when independent patches
may be superseded, or have to be tediously reworked and reintegrated
into the game, often over much time.

This isn't an ecology.

 
> Writing software libraries and writing games are two completely
> different things and their development is different.
> 
> The goal of software libraries is way more technical and to find a
> consensus what to do is much easier than with games. It is not
> surprising that there are much more good open source software
> libraries out there than good open source games. 


You may be right that gamers tend to squabble more.  But so what? Good
project management gets results.  The alternative we have now -
"everybody just go off independently and do their own thing" - might
have a certain libertarian appeal to some people, but it's not helping
the community.

 
> Why do you think that those contributions would have been incorporated
> into the official version when the DevTeam would act like a normal
> open source development team? Even Linus doesn't accept everything
> that the developer wants to be in the linux kernel.
> 
> When the goals of the developer are different they have to be on
> different projects.


Of course not everything would be accepted.  That's not what I'm
saying.  But overall, more new ideas would be integrated.  There would
be a much higher degree of organization and communication.  Work would
be less duplicated and accomplished more quickly.  Releases wouldn't
be glacial.


> I doubt that. When no one wants to do it, why should that be easier
> with central, coordinated (who would coordinate) development?
> Especially with a huge undertaking like OO-nethack. You can't do that
> in small incremental steps.


Everything can be broken down into incremental steps - that's what
software is all about.

Teamwork accomplishes amazing things.  The lone wolf programmer model
isn't delivering here.


> So what? SlashEm is not the official "NetHack", NetHack is not the
> official "Hack", Hack wasn't the official "Rogue".


Of course the devteam are the de facto "official" maintainers of
nethack!  That's why everyone waits around for them for years to
release something, patches are applied to their releases, etc. etc.

